  [PASS] R0 [control] the baryon fraction recovered from these rows lands in the observed cluster range 0.08-0.18   (median f_bar = 0.149, range [0.115, 0.160])
  [PASS] R1 [cosmic] the Newtonian dark-to-baryon ratio at the outermost radius is consistent with the cosmic value allowing 0-30% baryon depletion (5.4-8.0)   (alt median 5.73, canonical median 5.73; cosmic 5.43, depleted-30% 7.76)
  [PASS] R2 [universal] that ratio is nearly universal across clusters (fractional scatter below 30%)   (alt 12%, canonical 12%)
  [FAIL] R3 [framework] the framework's residual is consistent with its own prediction of zero   (alt median 2.76 M_bar, 13 sigma from 0, canonical median 3.09 M_bar, 15 sigma from 0)
  [FAIL] R4 [trend] the framework's residual does not grow systematically with radius (a residual that tracks acceleration is a wrong kernel, not a mass)   (alt -1.97 per dex, canonical -1.90 per dex)
  [FAIL] R5 [verdict] the framework's reading of clusters is at least as economical as the cosmic-share reading   (the Newtonian reading needs ONE number that is not fitted here -- the cosmic ratio, known independently from the CMB and BBN -- and reproduces the clusters with the scatter quoted in R2; the framework's reading needs a residual it predicts to be zero)

  what this does and does not show: it does NOT measure dark matter, and it does not touch the galaxy-scale
  evidence, where baryons plus the kernel work and a cosmic-share halo would overshoot (g04k: 2.6 M_b inside 10 kpc
  against the 0.25 M_b the RAR tolerates).  It shows only which reading of the CLUSTER rows is the more economical,
  and that the number clusters ask for is one that LambdaCDM fixes independently rather than fits.

RESULT: 3 FAIL -> ["R3 [framework] the framework's residual is consistent with its own prediction of zero", "R4 [trend] the framework's residual does not grow systematically with radius (a residual that tracks acceleration is a wrong kernel, not a mass)", "R5 [verdict] the framework's reading of clusters is at least as economical as the cosmic-share reading"]
